\section{Give a Dataflow Run Its Own Numbering}

Solve backward liveness on the reachable blocks of a fixed
function. To avoid ambiguities about joins, assume in this
chapter that the IR has ordinary local variables and no SSA
edge arguments. Within a block, \(\mathrm{use}\) contains variables
read before their first definition and \(\mathrm{def}\) contains all
variables defined. At function exit there is no implicit live
variable in this example: return operands are ordinary uses
inside the return block. Then \(\mathrm{out}(b)\) is the union of
\(\mathrm{in}\) over successors and
\(\mathrm{in}(b)=\mathrm{use}(b)\cup
(\mathrm{out}(b)\setminus\mathrm{def}(b))\). Muchnick separates
these transfer operations from the worklist management used to
find a fixed point \cite{muchnick1997advanced}.

Build a dense numbering of active variables and another of
blocks. Their indices are not generational IR handles or
interner IDs. A bit array sized by the largest symbol index
in a whole workspace is wasteful when a function names only
twenty variables. Associate saved results with the numbered
domain and graph revision. Two equal vectors from different
numberings need not denote the same set of variables, and a
new edge may invalidate facts even if the numbering still fits.

\section{Choose a Container for Each Kind of Set}

A dense bitset for \(n\) elements on \(w\)-bit words stores
\(\lceil n/w\rceil\) words. Whole-set union, difference, and
change detection use word operations, with unused tail bits
kept zero; complement must mask them. A classic sparse set
instead maintains \texttt{dense[0:size]} for enumeration and
\texttt{sparse[id]} for candidate positions into that prefix.
Membership tests both the bounds and \texttt{dense[pos] == id}.
Ignoring the equality check lets an old position claim
membership for an element that was cleared or swapped out.
Initialize any storage that may be read, even if the sparse
positions are not semantically meaningful until checked.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Container & Attractive operation & Cost or caveat \\
\hline
Dense bitset & Wordwise union and subtraction & Touches words even when empty \\
Sparse set & Constant-time insert, erase, clear & Index array scales with domain \\
Sorted sparse vector & Merge two small sorted sets & Random insertion can shift entries \\
Queue and queued flags & Ordered pop and deduplication & Maintain two representations \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Sparse-set erase normally swaps the last dense element into
the hole and repairs its sparse position. Clear may just set
size to zero when entries are plain indices, not owners of
resources. This is useful for temporary sets that see only a
few of a fixed universe in each use. It is not automatically
a memory win to store a sparse set per block: if each one
has an \(n\)-entry sparse index, storage can dwarf bitsets.
Keep the sparse index for scratch sets or membership in the
pending worklist, and choose the stored liveness representation
by cardinality and union workload.

\section{Send Changes Against the Direction of Control}

In \Cref{fig:visual-425}, entry \(E\) defines \(x\), header
\(H\) uses \(x\), loop body \(B\) uses \(y\), and exit \(R\)
returns \(x\). Assume \(y\) is an incoming function parameter,
not defined by an instruction in \(E\). Once \(B\)'s live-in
gains \(y\), \(H\) must reconsider its live-out, and so must
\(E\) if \(H\)'s live-in changes. A solver that only wakes
successors cannot guarantee either update. The fixed points
for \(H\) and \(B\) both contain \(x,y\) on entry; \(E\)
has \(y\), not \(x\), live on entry, since it defines \(x\).

