\section{Deduplicate the Worklist Without Losing a Self Update}

\begin{verbatim}
schedule(b):
    if queued[b]: return
    queued[b] := true
    queue.push(b)

solve_liveness(blocks):
    for b in blocks:
        in[b] := empty_bits()
        out[b] := empty_bits()
        queued[b] := false
    for b in initial_order(blocks):
        schedule(b)
    while not queue.empty():
        b := queue.pop()
        queued[b] := false
        next_out := empty_bits()
        for s in successors[b]:
            next_out.or_into(in[s])
        next_in := use[b] | (next_out & ~def[b])
        out[b] := next_out
        if next_in != in[b]:
            in[b] := next_in
            for p in predecessors[b]:
                schedule(p)
    return (in, out)
\end{verbatim}

The flag is cleared before transfer so that a self-edge can
schedule the current block if needed. Both tables are stored,
but only changed live-in influences another block's equation.
Bitset ownership matters: assigning \texttt{out[b]} must not
publish a scratch array that the next iteration wipes. Seed
every block in the domain, including a reachable nonterminating
loop with no path from it to an exit. Starting only at return
blocks would miss the loop's local reads. Initialize from the
empty sets for this may-analysis: with monotone transfers and
a finite domain bits only grow at successive iterations, and
each strict increase can wake finitely many predecessors.

\section{Benchmark Iterations, Footprint, and Invalidation}

Queue or stack order shouldn't change the least fixed point
in this setup, but it changes the number of transfers. A
postorder over the forward CFG often gives successor results
to predecessors sooner; if scheduling by a traversal of the
reverse graph, remember components that cannot reach a return.
Compare results against a full-sweep solver on the same graph.
In the loop fixture, assert \(y\) reaches \(E\) and \(x\)
does not. Include a self-loop whose live-out changes after
its first pop, a domain one greater than the word width, and
a test that clears and then repopulates the same sparse set.

Count word operations, pushes suppressed by membership,
revisits, and reserved bytes per block. With 256 dense
variables, one row costs 32 bytes for the bits; a sparse set
with a dedicated 32-bit index per variable costs 1024 bytes
before its dense array. At much larger domains where nearly
all sets remain tiny, a sorted vector may avoid touching long
zero spans. Neither choice excuses stale facts after edits.
Deleting a use can shrink the desired fixed point; merely
seeding work with old nonempty rows is not guaranteed to
recover the least solution of cyclic equations. The simple
safe baseline is to clear and solve again on the changed graph.

\section{Validate the Sparse Set Without Clearing Its Index Array}

\begin{verbatim}
contains(set, id):
    if id outside set.domain: return false
    p := set.sparse[id]
    return 0 <= p < set.size and set.dense[p] == id

insert(set, id):
    require id belongs to set.domain
    if contains(set, id): return false
    set.dense[set.size] := id
    set.sparse[id] := set.size
    set.size += 1
    return true

erase(set, id):
    if not contains(set, id): return false
    hole := set.sparse[id]
    last := set.dense[set.size - 1]
    set.dense[hole] := last
    set.sparse[last] := hole
    set.size -= 1
    return true
\end{verbatim}

For a trace, insert 20 and 31, clear by setting size to
zero, and then insert 7. The stale index for 20 points to
position zero, which is now in bounds again. Only the
equality check rejects membership. Separately erase 20
from the original two-element set. Repairing the moved
31's index is necessary even though both dense elements
still exist as initialized memory. This representation
can schedule work in arbitrary order by popping the last
dense member, but it cannot stand in for a FIFO queue if
the algorithm measures or relies on a particular order.

A liveness diagnostic might enumerate variables set in a
row. Wordwise iteration can skip zero words and remove the
least-significant set bit from a local word copy per emitted
index. On 64-bit words, test index 63, 64, and an empty
last word; this catches shifts carried out in a narrower
host type or indices produced from padding. Compare the
enumeration against the solver's reference set and verify
that formatting the diagnostic did not mutate the row.
